cartridge "ASTRO-PANIC!/Charles Brannon/1984"

' ASTRO-PANIC!
' A ColecoVision game based on "Astro-Panic!" by Charles Brannon,
' COMPUTE!'s Gazette February 1984 (Commodore 64 machine language; a
' VIC-20 version followed on the April 1985 disk).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules and numbers are the original's (read
' from the C64 machine code, orig/ap64.dis, and watched in jsc64), rebuilt
' for the ColecoVision: smooth sprites, a star field, sound.
'
' The game: seven flying saucers swarm over your laser base. Shoot them all
' to clear the wave; the next wave is faster. A saucer that dives onto the
' base destroys it, and the whole swarm of seven comes back.
'
' From the original (orig/ap64.dis): the game keeps the C64's sprite
' coordinates (saucers x 32-319, y 50-209; the base x 24-320 at y 212) and
' maps them to the screen through XMap / YMap. The base moves 2 a frame.
' One game pass moves the laser shot one character row up (20 rows, then
' it is gone; one shot at a time, fired from the base's character column)
' and every saucer by its dx and dy (each 1 or 3, either way, drawn at
' random); a saucer that would leave y 50-209 or x 32-319 draws a new
' random dx and dy instead of moving (and a stopped x move still keeps its
' y move). The passes run at the C64's rate (its delay loop, 15 x 256
' loops at level 0 and one less each level): 0.84 passes a frame at level
' 0, 1.49 at level 7. A saucer hit scores (255 - its y) / 8, 5 to 25
' points, high saucers more, and the game stops for 10 frames per saucer
' hit (one shot can hit several). All seven down: 100 points, the level
' goes up and a new wave starts in a row at y 60, the base in the middle.
' A saucer touching the base (the C64's sprite collision, here by the two
' shapes' widths line by line, HitW / SHw) costs a life (3) and restarts
' the wave of seven. Score and high score, six digits.
'
' Liberties: the passes per frame stop growing as fast from level 8 (1.63
' at level 8, 2.0 from level 12; the C64 reached 7 a frame at level 14,
' then crawled at level 15 when its delay counter reached 0 and looped 256
' times); levels go on to 99; a burst sprite instead of the C64's
' random-colour flashing (and a steady colour for the shot), so nothing
' flashes faster than 3 Hz; a pause of 3/4 s after a cleared wave and 1 s
' when the base is hit (the C64's busy-loop sound effects); stars and the
' ground; GAME OVER then the title (the C64 waited for F7); no shift-key
' pause; a base that has sat still for 4 seconds (240 frames) turns grey
' and scores nothing (no hit points, no wave bonus) until it moves again
' (on the C64, standing in the middle and holding fire scored better than
' playing). Own xorshift random numbers (the C64 read the SID's noise).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the base left and right,
' either button fires (hold it to keep firing).
' The title has a menu (stick up/down, a button): START, HOW TO PLAY
' (three pages) and CREDITS.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once.

const TSky = 128
const TStar1 = 129
const TStar2 = 130
const TStar3 = 131
const TGnd = 132
const TLife = 133
const TTb1 = 134
const TTb2 = 135
const TTb3 = 136
const SShipd = 0
